**Four indicted in fatal Deutsche Bank fire as New York overhauls construction-site safety**

**NEW YORK —** A Manhattan grand jury brought homicide and reckless-endangerment charges against the demolition contractor John Galt Corp. and three construction supervisors following the 2007 fire at the former Deutsche Bank building in Lower Manhattan, where firefighters **Robert Beddia and Joseph Graffagnino** died after crews encountered a disabled standpipe, blocked passageways and a building filled with combustible debris.

John Galt Corp.; **Jeffrey Melofchik**, the site-safety manager for construction manager Bovis Lend Lease; and John Galt supervisors **Mitchel Alvo and Salvatore DePaola** were charged with **two counts each of second-degree manslaughter and criminally negligent homicide**, along with **second-degree reckless endangerment**. The manslaughter counts alleged that reckless conduct caused the two deaths; the negligent-homicide counts offered prosecutors an alternative theory based on criminal negligence. The defendants pleaded not guilty.

The charges followed a 16-month investigation into the Aug. 18, 2007, blaze at **130 Liberty Street**, a 41-story tower damaged and contaminated in the Sept. 11 attacks. The building was being stripped of hazardous materials and dismantled floor by floor.

Prosecutors said the defendants allowed work to proceed despite a collection of life-threatening conditions. Most critically, a section of the building’s **standpipe—the vertical water main firefighters rely on in high-rises—had been removed in the basement months before the fire**. The break went undetected or unreported despite inspection and safety obligations. When firefighters connected hoses and tried to send water upward, they could not obtain an effective supply.

Investigators also described widespread disregard of the site’s no-smoking rules. Workers allegedly smoked inside the building, and supervisors failed to stop the practice. Authorities believed smoking materials probably ignited combustible debris, although they did not identify the person who started the fire.

The tower’s interior compounded the danger. Plastic sheeting used for asbestos containment, plywood, construction materials and accumulated debris helped the fire spread and produced thick smoke. Temporary partitions and sealed or obstructed openings created a maze for firefighters. Prosecutors said required housekeeping, egress and fire-protection inspections were either inadequate or falsely documented.

Beddia and Graffagnino became trapped on the 14th floor while searching for the seat of the fire. With the standpipe unusable, firefighters had to stretch hose lines up the tower from outside water sources, losing critical time. Both men died from smoke inhalation.

The indictment did not accuse the defendants of intentionally causing the fire. Rather, prosecutors argued that the known and continuing safety violations—particularly the missing standpipe section, failure to control smoking and failure to maintain clear, safe working areas—created an unjustifiable risk of death.

The disaster also exposed serious weaknesses in city oversight. The building had been inspected repeatedly by various agencies, yet no single system ensured that the Fire Department, Buildings Department and environmental regulators shared current information about fire-protection systems and changing site conditions. Inspections shortly before the fire failed to reveal the severed standpipe and other hazards.

In response, New York City adopted a series of reforms for buildings under construction, alteration or demolition. The changes included:

- more frequent, specialized Fire Department inspections of high-rise construction and demolition sites;
- stricter testing and documentation of standpipes and sprinkler systems;
- immediate notification to the Fire Department when a fire-protection system is impaired;
- clearer responsibility for designated construction-site fire-safety managers and impairment coordinators;
- stronger enforcement of smoking bans, debris removal and unobstructed exits;
- improved information-sharing between the Fire Department and Buildings Department;
- requirements intended to make temporary standpipes easier to identify and monitor, including pressurization or alarm systems capable of revealing breaks or unauthorized removal.

The city also created dedicated inspection and enforcement efforts for high-risk construction and demolition properties and revised its fire code to place greater emphasis on active fire protection throughout demolition—not merely in occupied buildings.

The criminal case later produced mixed results: the individual defendants were acquitted, while John Galt Corp. was convicted of reckless endangerment but cleared of the homicide counts. Nevertheless, the fire became a defining construction-safety failure for New York, demonstrating how routine violations, fragmented oversight and the loss of a single critical water system could turn a building-site fire into a fatal emergency.
